0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 222 10 "Problem 3." }{TEXT 282 3 ".  " }}
{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 282 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 282 56 "There i
s a one-to-one correspondence between subsets of " }{TEXT 223 17 "\{1,
 2, . . . , n\}" }{TEXT 282 28 " and binary lists of length " }{TEXT 
224 1 "n" }{TEXT 282 17 ", that is, lists " }{TEXT 225 3 "L =" }{TEXT 
282 1 " " }{TEXT 226 22 "[x1, x2 , . . . , xn] " }{TEXT 282 6 "where "
 }{TEXT 227 18 "x1, x2, . . . , xn" }{TEXT 282 25 " are elements of th
e set " }{TEXT 228 5 "\{0,1\}" }{TEXT 282 57 ".  The correspondence is
 given by associating to the set " }{TEXT 229 1 "S" }{TEXT 282 10 " th
e list " }{TEXT 230 1 "L" }{TEXT 282 7 " where " }{TEXT 231 34 "xi = 1
 if i is in S and 0 if not. " }{TEXT 282 78 "For example, the set \{1,
3,5\} corresponds to the list [1,0,1,0,1,0,0] if n = 7." }}{PARA 0 "" 
0 "" {TEXT 282 1 "\n" }{TEXT 232 3 "(a)" }{TEXT 282 19 " Write a proce
dure " }{TEXT 233 12 "list_to_set " }{TEXT 282 79 "whose input is a bi
nary list and whose output is the corresponding set. E. g., " }{TEXT 
234 24 "list_to_set([1,0,1,0,1])" }{TEXT 282 21 " will return the set 
" }{TEXT 235 7 "\{1,3,5\}" }{TEXT 282 44 ". Note that nops(L) is the l
ength of a list." }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 282 1 "\n" }{TEXT 236 3 "(b)
" }{TEXT 282 19 " Write a procedure " }{TEXT 237 11 "set_to_list" }
{TEXT 282 22 " whose input is a pair" }{TEXT 238 4 " S,n" }{TEXT 282 
7 " where " }{TEXT 239 1 "S" }{TEXT 282 16 " is a subset of " }{TEXT 
240 17 "\{1, 2, . . . , n\}" }{TEXT 282 5 " and " }{TEXT 241 1 "n" }
{TEXT 282 69 " is a positive integer and whose output is the binary li
st of length " }{TEXT 242 1 "n" }{TEXT 282 26 " corresponding to the s
et " }{TEXT 243 1 "S" }{TEXT 282 23 ". E. g., if n = 5 then " }{TEXT 
244 22 "set_to_list(\{1,3,5\},5)" }{TEXT 282 13 " will return " }{TEXT
 245 11 "[1,0,1,0,1]" }{TEXT 282 2 ".\n" }{TEXT 282 1 "\n" }{TEXT 246 
3 "(c)" }{TEXT 282 59 " Show by a few tests that each procedure works.
 Then apply " }{TEXT 247 11 "set_to_list" }{TEXT 282 32 " to each set \+
in the powerset of " }{TEXT 248 12 "\{1, 2, 3, 4\}" }{TEXT 282 158 " t
o form all binary lists of length 4. Make a program to print out a tab
le of the following form. (But the order need not be the same as that \+
started below.)\n" }{TEXT 282 1 "\n" }{TEXT 282 24 "   [0,0,0,0] <--> \+
 \{  \}\n" }{TEXT 282 24 "   [1,0,0 0] <--> \{ 1 \}\n" }{TEXT 282 24 "
   [0,1,0,0] <--> \{ 2 \} " }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 282 12 "    ......
.." }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 282 7 "    etc" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 
282 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 282 108 "Some extra commas in the out
put is okay. You may obtain the power set of the set \{1,2,...,n\} by \+
the command " }{TEXT 249 12 "powerset(n);" }{TEXT 282 37 " but you mus
t first load the package " }{TEXT 250 9 "combinat." }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 
"> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 28 "list_to_set:=proc(n::list);\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 
0 12 "a:=nops(n);\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 7 "S:=\{\};\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 14 "fo
r i to a do\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 40 "if n[i]=1 then S:= S union \{i\}; end
 if;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 8 "end do;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 10 "return S;\n" }
{MPLTEXT 1 0 9 "end proc:" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 1 "\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 25 "list
_to_set([1,0,1,0,1]);" }}{PARA 207 "" 1 "" {TEXT 283 0 "" }{HYPERLNK 
283 "Warning, `a` is implicitly declared local to procedure `list_to_s
et`" 4 "http://www.maplesoft.com/support/help/errors/view.aspx?path=Wa
rning,%20%60a%60%20is%20implicitly%20declared%20local%20to%20procedure
%20%60list_to_set%60" "" }{TEXT 283 0 "" }}{PARA 207 "" 1 "" {TEXT 
283 0 "" }{HYPERLNK 283 "Warning, `S` is implicitly declared local to \+
procedure `list_to_set`" 4 "http://www.maplesoft.com/support/help/erro
rs/view.aspx?path=Warning,%20%60S%60%20is%20implicitly%20declared%20lo
cal%20to%20procedure%20%60list_to_set%60" "" }{TEXT 283 0 "" }}{PARA 
207 "" 1 "" {TEXT 283 0 "" }{HYPERLNK 283 "Warning, `i` is implicitly \+
declared local to procedure `list_to_set`" 4 "http://www.maplesoft.com
/support/help/errors/view.aspx?path=Warning,%20%60i%60%20is%20implicit
ly%20declared%20local%20to%20procedure%20%60list_to_set%60" "" }{TEXT 
283 0 "" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "<%\"\"\"\"\"$\"\"&" }}}
{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 251 11 "Problem  4." }{TEXT 282 1 " " }}
{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 282 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 282 13 "Note th
at if " }{TEXT 252 1 "i" }{TEXT 282 20 " is an integer then " }{TEXT 
253 18 "convert(i,base, 2)" }{TEXT 282 14 " will convert " }{TEXT 254 
2 "i " }{TEXT 282 47 "to a binary list (the binary representation of "
 }{TEXT 255 1 "i" }{TEXT 282 37 " in reverse order). Then you may use 
" }{TEXT 256 11 "list_to_set" }{TEXT 282 46 " to convert  this list to
 a set. Note that if " }{TEXT 257 1 "i" }{TEXT 282 11 " runs from " }
{TEXT 258 1 "0" }{TEXT 282 4 " to " }{XPPEDIT 204 0 "Typesetting:-mrow
(Typesetting:-mi(\"\"), Typesetting:-mrow(Typesetting:-msup(Typesettin
g:-mn(\"2\", font_style_name = \"CharacterStyle256\", mathvariant = \"
normal\"), Typesetting:-mi(\"n\", italic = \"true\", font_style_name =
 \"CharacterStyle256\", mathvariant = \"italic\"), superscriptshift = \+
\"0\"), Typesetting:-mo(\"&minus;\", font_style_name = \"CharacterStyl
e256\", mathvariant = \"normal\", fence = \"false\", separator = \"fal
se\", stretchy = \"false\", symmetric = \"false\", largeop = \"false\"
, movablelimits = \"false\", accent = \"false\", lspace = \"0.2222222e
m\", rspace = \"0.2222222em\"), Typesetting:-mn(\"1\", font_style_name
 = \"CharacterStyle256\", mathvariant = \"normal\")), Typesetting:-mi(
\"\"));" "-I%mrowG6#/I+modulenameG6\"I,TypesettingGI(_syslibGF'6%-I#mi
GF$6#Q!F'-F#6%-I%msupGF$6%-I#mnGF$6%Q\"2F'/%0font_style_nameGQ2Charact
erStyle256F'/%,mathvariantGQ'normalF'-F,6&Q\"nF'/%'italicGQ%trueF'F8/F
<Q'italicF'/%1superscriptshiftGQ\"0F'-I#moGF$6.Q(&minus;F'F8F;/%&fence
GQ&falseF'/%*separatorGFO/%)stretchyGFO/%*symmetricGFO/%(largeopGFO/%.
movablelimitsGFO/%'accentGFO/%'lspaceGQ,0.2222222emF'/%'rspaceGFhn-F56
%Q\"1F'F8F;F+" }{TEXT 282 6 " then " }{TEXT 259 32 "list_to_set(conver
t(i,base,2) ) " }{TEXT 282 33 " will run through all subsets of " }
{TEXT 260 16 "\{1, 2, . . ., n\}" }{TEXT 282 3 ". \n" }{TEXT 282 1 "\n
" }{TEXT 261 3 "(a)" }{TEXT 282 42 " Use this idea to make your own pr
ocedure " }{TEXT 262 9 "PowerSet " }{TEXT 282 17 "which will given " }
{TEXT 263 1 "n" }{TEXT 282 27 ",  produce the powerset of " }{TEXT 
264 17 "\{1, 2, . . . , n\}" }{TEXT 282 87 ". Show by several examples
 that your procedure works. For your examples you should get " }{TEXT 
265 24 "nops(PowerSet(n)) = 2^(n" }{TEXT 282 32 "). Check that this is
 the case.\n" }{TEXT 282 2 " \n" }{TEXT 266 3 "(b)" }{TEXT 282 35 " Us
e this idea to make a procedure " }{TEXT 267 7 "RandSet" }{TEXT 282 
43 " which given n produces a random subset of " }{TEXT 268 17 "\{1, 2
, . . . , n\}" }{TEXT 282 7 ". [Use " }{TEXT 269 4 "rand" }{TEXT 282 
42 " to produce a random integer in the range " }{TEXT 270 8 "0..2^n-1
" }{TEXT 282 36 " and then convert it to a subset of " }{TEXT 271 17 "
\{1, 2, . . . , n\}" }{TEXT 282 1 "." }{TEXT 272 3 "]  " }{TEXT 273 
31 "Do NOT use PowerSet or powerset" }{TEXT 282 240 ". Show by example
s that it works for small n such as 5, 10, and 20 as well as for large
 n such as 100. It will even work for n = 1000, but the output will be
 rather large. On average a random subset of \{1,2,...,n\} will contai
n n/2 elements." }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 274 7 "[Remark" }{TEXT 282 
62 ": The powerset of a set with 1000 elements contains more than " }
{XPPEDIT 18 0 "Typesetting:-mrow(Typesetting:-mi(\"\"), Typesetting:-m
row(Typesetting:-msup(Typesetting:-mn(\"10\", executable = \"false\", \+
mathvariant = \"normal\"), Typesetting:-mn(\"300\", executable = \"fal
se\", mathvariant = \"normal\"), superscriptshift = \"0\")), Typesetti
ng:-mi(\"\"));" "-I%mrowG6#/I+modulenameG6\"I,TypesettingGI(_syslibGF'
6%-I#miGF$6#Q!F'-F#6#-I%msupGF$6%-I#mnGF$6%Q#10F'/%+executableGQ&false
F'/%,mathvariantGQ'normalF'-F56%Q$300F'F8F;/%1superscriptshiftGQ\"0F'F
+" }{TEXT 282 171 " elements. This is, according to current estimates,
 more than the number of atoms in the whole universe. So you cannot ex
pect your computer to be able to store such a set." }{TEXT 275 1 "]" }
}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 0 "" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 ""
 {TEXT 204 10 "Problem 5." }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 282 0 "" }}{PARA 0 
"" 0 "" {TEXT 282 105 "Construct an original example of each of the fo
llowing types of Maple expressions. Check each using both " }{TEXT 
276 4 "type" }{TEXT 282 5 " and " }{TEXT 277 10 "whattype. " }{TEXT 
282 25 "Note that in the case of " }{TEXT 278 8 "whattype" }{TEXT 282 
21 " you may need to use " }{TEXT 279 4 "eval" }{TEXT 282 151 " as ill
ustrated in the lecture. To separate the examples make a line using da
shes like the following (in text mode) between the examples for each t
ype." }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 282 70 "--------------------------------
